        "dependency_level": 12,
        "statement": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $q\\in R^{\\times}$, $d,n\\ge0$, and $H=H_{R,q}(S_n)$ ([[def-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra]]). Let $\\Lambda(d,n)$ be the set of weak compositions $\\mu=(\\mu_1,\\dots,\\mu_d)$ of $n$ (so $\\mu_i\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ and $\\sum_i\\mu_i=n$; zero parts are allowed and give empty rows). For $\\mu\\in\\Lambda(d,n)$ let $m_\\mu=\\sum_{w\\in S_\\mu}T_w$ ([[def-murphy-elements-and-murphy-tableau-elements]]) and let $M^{\\mu}:=m_\\mu H$ be the right ideal it generates, the **(Hecke) permutation module**; $M^{\\mu}$ is free over $R$ with basis $\\{m_\\mu T_{d(t)}:t\\text{ row-standard }\\mu\\text{-tableau}\\}$ and is the Hecke analogue of the module induced from the trivial representation of the Young subgroup ([[def-young-subgroup-tabloid-and-permutation-module]]).\n\nFor a weak composition $\\mu\\in\\Lambda(d,n)$, define $T_0(\\lambda,\\mu)$ to be the fillings of $[\\lambda]$ by $1,\\ldots,d$ with exactly $\\mu_i$ entries equal to $i$, weakly increasing rows and strictly increasing columns. Put $T_0(\\lambda)=\\bigcup_{\\mu\\in\\Lambda(d,n)}T_0(\\lambda,\\mu)$ and $K_{\\lambda\\mu}:=|T_0(\\lambda,\\mu)|$. This explicitly extends the partition-content definition of [[def-semistandard-tableau-and-kostka-number]] to weak compositions, including internal zero counts; a zero count means that its label is absent. The set is finite, since at most $d^n$ fillings occur for $n>0$, and at $n=0$ its unique member is the empty filling.\n\nHere is the freeness statement in detail: the cosets $S_\\mu d$ with $d\\in D_\\mu$ partition $S_n$, and $\\ell(ud)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$ for $u\\in S_\\mu$, $d\\in D_\\mu$ ([[def-murphy-elements-and-murphy-tableau-elements]]), so the standard basis theorem ([[thm-standard-basis-of-the-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra]]) gives $T_uT_{d(t)}=T_{ud(t)}$ and $m_\\mu T_{d(t)}=\\sum_{u\\in S_\\mu}T_{u\\,d(t)}$; these are the standard basis elements indexed by the single coset $S_\\mu d(t)$, and the cosets are disjoint, whence $M^\\mu=\\bigoplus_tR\\,m_\\mu T_{d(t)}$ is free with the displayed basis as $t$ runs over the row-standard $\\mu$-tableaux.\n\nPut $M:=\\bigoplus_{\\mu\\in\\Lambda(d,n)}M^{\\mu}$ ([[def-direct-sum-of-a-family-of-modules]]) and define the **q-Schur algebra** $$S(d,n):=\\mathrm{End}_H(M),$$ the $R$-algebra of right $H$-module endomorphisms of $M$ ([[def-left-and-right-modules]]).\n\n**Cellular structure.** For all $\\mu,\\nu\\in\\Lambda(d,n)$, $\\mathrm{Hom}_H(M^{\\nu},M^{\\mu})$ is free with the semistandard-pair basis constructed below; for every partition $\\lambda$ of $n$ with at most $d$ parts and all semistandard $\\lambda$-tableaux $S,T$ there is an element $\\varphi_{ST}\\in S(d,n)$ ([[def-semistandard-tableau-and-kostka-number]]), and the family $$\\{\\varphi_{ST}:\\lambda\\in\\Lambda^{+}(d,n),\\ S,T\\in T_0(\\lambda)\\},$$ where $\\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$ is the set of partitions of $n$ with at most $d$ parts ordered by dominance $\\unrhd$ ([[def-dominance-order-on-partitions]]), is an $R$-basis of $S(d,n)$; the map $\\varphi_{ST}\\mapsto\\varphi_{TS}$ is an algebra anti-automorphism and the triangular axiom holds modulo the span of the $\\varphi_{UV}$ with $\\mu\\rhd\\lambda$. Hence $(\\varphi,\\Lambda^{+}(d,n))$ is a dominance-cellular basis of $S(d,n)$ ([[def-cellular-algebra-basis-and-cell-module]]) and its cell modules are the **Weyl modules** $W^{\\lambda}$, free over $R$ with basis $\\{\\varphi_T:T\\in T_0(\\lambda)\\}$. Over a field $F$, $W^{\\lambda}$ has nonzero simple head $L^{\\lambda}$, the $L^{\\lambda}$ for $\\lambda\\in\\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$ form a complete set of pairwise non-isomorphic absolutely irreducible $S(d,n)$-modules, and the decomposition matrix satisfies $d_{\\lambda\\lambda}=1$ and $d_{\\lambda\\mu}\\ne0\\Rightarrow\\lambda\\unrhd\\mu$.\n\n**Explicit normalization of the semistandard maps.** For a standard $\\lambda$-tableau $s$, let $\\mu(s)$ replace each entry by the row of that entry in the canonical $\\mu$-tableau. For semistandard $S,T$ of respective types $\\mu,\\nu$, put $$m_{ST}:=\\sum_{\\substack{s,t\\in\\operatorname{Std}(\\lambda)\\\\ \\mu(s)=S,\\ \\nu(t)=T}}m_{st}.$$ The normalized basis map $\\varphi_{ST}$ is zero on the other summands and sends $m_\\nu h\\in M^\\nu$ to $m_{ST}h\\in M^\\mu$. The intersection $m_{ST}\\in M^\\mu\\cap(M^\\nu)^*$ makes this well-defined: if $m_\\nu h=0$, write $m_{ST}=a m_\\nu$ and obtain $m_{ST}h=0$. The integral intersection and Hom construction below proves that these are exactly the Hom basis; their composition is ordinary composition of endomorphisms. For the regular type $\\omega=(1^n)$ the fibers are singleton standard tableaux, so evaluation at $1$ identifies the corner map with $m_{st}$. This fixes the actual module-and-form normalization used by the corner and weight-Gram calculations (Mathas (4.13), with Corollary 4.12 and Theorem 4.14). No quantum factorial is inverted in this definition.\n\n**Weight idempotents and weight spaces.** For each composition $\\nu\\in\\Lambda(d,n)$, let $1_\\nu\\in\\operatorname{End}_H(M)$ be the identity on the summand $M^\\nu$ and zero on every other summand. These weight projections satisfy $$1_\\nu^2=1_\\nu,\\qquad 1_\\mu1_\\nu=0\\ (\\mu\\ne\\nu),\\qquad   \\sum_\\nu1_\\nu=1.$$ They are not all of the diagonal cellular elements $\\varphi_{TT}$: a general cellular diagonal is not asserted idempotent or orthogonal. For a partition $\\nu$ the canonical tableau $T^\\nu$ of shape and type $\\nu$ gives $1_\\nu=\\varphi_{T^\\nu T^\\nu}$; for a general composition the projection is defined directly as above, without pretending that an arbitrary composition is a partition-shaped semistandard tableau. The basis map $\\varphi_{ST}$ is supported between the summands indicated by the types of $S$ and $T$. Thus the fixed-first-index Weyl basis vector $\\varphi_T$ survives right multiplication by $1_\\nu$ exactly when $T$ has type $\\nu$. Over any field, $$\\dim_F(W^\\lambda1_\\nu)=|T_0(\\lambda,\\nu)|=K_{\\lambda\\nu}.$$ This follows by applying the projection to the displayed free Weyl basis, not by assuming every diagonal cellular element is a projection ([[def-semistandard-tableau-and-kostka-number]]).",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 The integral Hom test. The cyclic right module $M^\\nu$ has presentation $H/\\sum_i(T_i-q)H$, where $i$ ranges over the generators of $S_\\nu$. Indeed, a length-additive decomposition $w=ud$ reduces $T_w$ in this quotient to $q^{\\ell(u)}T_d$, and the map $T_d\\mapsto m_\\nu T_d$ maps the resulting spanning family bijectively to the independent disjoint-coset basis. Therefore evaluation at $m_\\nu$ identifies $\\operatorname{Hom}_H(M^\\nu,M^\\mu)$ with the elements $y\\in M^\\mu$ satisfying $yT_i=qy$ for those generators. Pairing $T_w,T_{ws_i}$ with $\\ell(ws_i)=\\ell(w)+1$ in the standard basis shows that this equation forces their two coefficients to be equal: the coefficient equations are $qb=qa$ and $a+(q-1)b=qb$, so $a=b$ since $q$ is a unit. Requiring all these equalities makes the coefficients constant on each right $S_\\nu$-coset. Its unweighted coset sum is $T_c m_\\nu$ for the minimal right-coset representative $c$, by length additivity. Hence this simultaneous right eigenspace is $Hm_\\nu=(M^\\nu)^*$, over every coefficient ring. Evaluation consequently gives an isomorphism $\\operatorname{Hom}_H(M^\\nu,M^\\mu)\\cong M^\\mu\\cap Hm_\\nu$. For $y=a m_\\nu$ its inverse is $m_\\nu h\\mapsto yh$, which is well defined because $m_\\nu h=0$ implies $yh=a m_\\nu h=0$. [given, F1, algebra, construct]\n\n2.1 The semistandard intersection. Expand $y\\in H$ uniquely in the Murphy basis. By [F2], membership in $M^\\mu$ requires zero coefficients outside semistandard first-index fibres and constant coefficients inside each fibre. Apply $*$ to [F2] to see that membership in $Hm_\\nu$ imposes the identical condition on the second-index fibres of type $\\nu$. The two requirements together say precisely that the coefficients are constant on each rectangle $\\{(s,t):\\mu(s)=S,\\nu(t)=T\\}$, and zero outside these rectangles. Thus their unweighted sums $m_{ST}$ form a basis of the intersection. The rectangles are disjoint and have a coefficient $1$ at every member, so independence and coefficient extraction hold over arbitrary $R$, including singular parameters; no quantum factorial is cancelled. Every nonempty rectangle has shape with at most $d$ rows, since a strictly increasing column can use only $d$ labels. Combining this with step 1.1 gives exactly the maps $\\varphi_{ST}$ and the asserted basis of each Hom block, hence of $S$. The construction also commutes with arbitrary base change, because the same free basis and formulas describe each specialized Hom block. [F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra, construct]\n\n3.1 The involution under composition. Define $f^*$ on a Hom block by replacing its evaluation element $y=f(m_\\nu)\\in M^\\mu\\cap Hm_\\nu$ by $y^*\\in M^\\nu\\cap Hm_\\mu$, and extend linearly to $S$. Step 2.1 gives $\\varphi_{ST}^*=\\varphi_{TS}$, so this is an involution. To check reversal, let $f:M^\\nu\\to M^\\mu$ have $f(m_\\nu)=a m_\\nu$ and $g:M^\\kappa\\to M^\\nu$ have $g(m_\\kappa)=b m_\\kappa=m_\\nu h$. Then $(fg)(m_\\kappa)=a b m_\\kappa$, so $(fg)^*(m_\\mu)=m_\\kappa b^*a^*$. On the other hand $f^*(m_\\mu)=m_\\nu a^*$ and $g^*(m_\\nu)=m_\\kappa b^*$, whence $(g^*f^*)(m_\\mu)=m_\\kappa b^*a^*$. Noncomposable blocks give zero on both sides. Since each block map is determined by its generating element, $(fg)^*=g^*f^*$ as claimed. [F1, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 The cellular multiplication law. Fix $T$ of shape $\\lambda$ and type $\\nu$, and first take $f:M^\\kappa\\to M^\\nu$. Write $f(m_\\kappa)=m_\\nu h$. Then $(\\varphi_{ST}f)(m_\\kappa)=m_{ST}h$. By [F3], its shape-$\\lambda$ Murphy coefficient at $(s,v)$ is $\\sum_{t:\\nu(t)=T}r_{tv}(h)$ if $\\mu(s)=S$, and zero otherwise; all remaining shapes strictly dominate $\\lambda$. This coefficient is independent of $s$. The full element lies in $M^\\mu\\cap Hm_\\kappa$ by step 1.1, so step 2.1 forces these coefficients to be constant on semistandard second-index fibres $\\kappa(v)=V$ and zero on the other fibres. For a fixed $T,h$, these constants are independent of $S$: choose any member $s$ of its nonempty first fibre and read the displayed sum, which contains no $s$. Calling the constants $r_V$, evaluation and the intersection basis give $\\varphi_{ST}f\\equiv\\sum_{V\\in T_0(\\lambda,\\kappa)}r_V\\varphi_{SV}$ modulo the span of strictly dominant Schur shapes, for every $S$. Sum over Hom blocks for arbitrary $f\\in S$. Step 3.1 gives the corresponding left law, so each upward span is a two-sided ideal and the cellular axioms hold. This proves cellularity by actual composition and Murphy coefficient comparison. [F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n5.1 Partition projections and nonzero forms. For the canonical semistandard tableau $T^\\lambda$ of shape and type $\\lambda$, its refinement fibre consists just of $t^\\lambda$, so $m_{T^\\lambda T^\\lambda}=m_\\lambda$. Accordingly $\\varphi_{T^\\lambda T^\\lambda}$ is the identity on $M^\\lambda$ and zero on other summands; it is the genuine projection $1_\\lambda$. Squaring it gives $1_\\lambda^2=1_\\lambda$. In the free shape-$\\lambda$ cell quotient, the cell product is $1_\\lambda^2\\equiv B_\\lambda(\\varphi_{T^\\lambda},\\varphi_{T^\\lambda})1_\\lambda$, and the coefficient of this basis vector is therefore $1$. Over a field this form is not identically zero. Step 4.1 and [F4] consequently show that every $W^\\lambda$ has the nonzero absolutely simple head $L^\\lambda$, that these heads give all simple modules exactly once, and that the stated diagonal and dominance decomposition conditions hold. [step 2.1, step 4.1, F4, algebra]\n\n6.1 Weight ranks and edge cases. Direct summand projections $1_\\nu$ satisfy the displayed orthogonality and sum-to-one relations. The source support of $\\varphi_{ST}$ implies $\\varphi_{ST}1_\\nu=\\delta_{\\operatorname{type}(T),\\nu}\\varphi_{ST}$, so the same formula holds on the right Weyl cell basis, proving its weight rank $K_{\\lambda\\nu}$. It asserts no idempotence for other cellular diagonal elements. Deleting empty rows leaves the Young subgroup unchanged and identifies the row-type fibres after the order-preserving relabelling of the remaining row labels; hence all constructions allow zero parts. At $n=0$ the unique empty tableau and empty partition give $H=R$, $S=R$ and cell form $1$. If $d=0<n$, the indexing set of weak compositions and the direct sum are empty, $S=0$, and every basis and simple-label assertion is empty. No division except by powers of the unit $q$, generic semisimplicity or choice principle enters any step. [given, step 2.1, step 4.1, step 5.1, algebra] \u220e",
