        "dependency_level": 13,
        "statement": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring with $1$, let $q\\in R^{\\times}$, let $d,n\\ge0$ with $d\\ge n$, and let $H=H_{R,q}(S_n)$ ([[def-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra]]). Write $S:=S(d,n)=\\mathrm{End}_H(M)$ for the type-A q-Schur algebra with $M=\\bigoplus_{\\mu\\in\\Lambda(d,n)}M^{\\mu}$ the direct sum of the permutation modules ([[def-type-a-q-schur-algebra-and-weyl-modules]], [[def-direct-sum-of-a-family-of-modules]]) and let $\\{\\varphi_{ST}:\\lambda\\in\\Lambda^{+}(d,n),\\ S,T\\in T_0(\\lambda)\\}$ be its cellular semistandard basis ([[def-murphy-elements-and-murphy-tableau-elements]] for the underlying Murphy elements and [[thm-standard-basis-of-the-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra]] for the standard basis along which this basis is defined). Put $\\omega:=(1^n,0^{d-n})\\in\\Lambda(d,n)$, the length-$d$ composition with $n$ entries equal to $1$ (legitimate because $d\\ge n$), so that the Young subgroup $S_\\omega$ is trivial and $m_\\omega=1$; hence $$M^{\\omega}=m_\\omega H=H$$ is the regular right $H$-module. Let $t^{\\omega}$ be the unique standard $\\omega$-tableau, with entries $1,2,\\dots,n$ down its single column; it is in particular the unique row-standard $\\omega$-tableau whose entries increase down the column. Set $$e:=\\varphi_{t^{\\omega}t^{\\omega}}\\in S.$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. $e$ is the projection of $M$ onto the direct summand $M^{\\omega}=H$ along the other summands $M^{\\mu}$, $\\mu\\ne\\omega$; in particular $e^2=e$ and $$eSe\\cong\\mathrm{End}_H(M^{\\omega})=\\mathrm{End}_H(H),$$ via the algebra isomorphism $$\\rho:H\\longrightarrow eSe,\\qquad h\\longmapsto\\rho_h,\\qquad \\rho_h(x):=hx,$$ left multiplication by $h$ on $M^{\\omega}=H$ being a right $H$-module endomorphism. Here the isomorphism $eSe\\cong\\mathrm{End}_H(M^{\\omega})$ sends $efe$ to $f|_{M^{\\omega}}$ and is inverted by extending an endomorphism of $M^{\\omega}$ by zero on the other summands, and the identification $\\mathrm{End}_H(H)\\cong H$ sends an endomorphism to its value at $1$. By the construction of the semistandard basis elements $\\varphi_{ST}$ (Mathas (4.13) with Corollaries 4.9 and 4.12; see the locator above), the diagonal element $\\varphi_{t^{\\omega}t^{\\omega}}$ is exactly the endomorphism of $M$ that is the identity on $M^{\\omega}$ and zero on all $M^{\\mu}$ with $\\mu\\ne\\omega$, which is the projection asserted above.\n\n2. The **Schur functor** is $$F:\\mathrm{mod}\\text{-}S\\longrightarrow\\mathrm{mod}\\text{-}H,\\qquad F(X):=Xe=X\\otimes_SSe,$$ with the right $H$-action transported along $\\rho$, so that $(xe)\\cdot h:=x\\,\\rho_h$ for $x\\in X$, $h\\in H$ ([[def-left-and-right-modules]]). It is exact and $R$-linear on morphisms: as a **left** $S$-module, $Se$ is the image of the left-$S$-linear projection $s\\mapsto se$ on $S$, hence is a direct summand of the regular left module and is projective. Tensoring right $S$-modules with this left module is therefore exact. Equivalently, for a short exact sequence of right modules, an element $ye$ in the target lifts to $xe$ from any lift $x$ of $y$; kernels and injections are likewise preserved by the same idempotent projection. The bimodule corner action makes these maps right $H$-linear. The convention is fixed: the functor is $X\\mapsto Xe$ on the right, and the left-module formula $X\\mapsto eX$ is not used on this page.",
