        "dependency_level": 13,
        "statement": "Let $F$ be a field, $q\\in F^{\\times}$, $d,n\\ge1$, and let $S=S(d,n)$ be the q-Schur algebra with Weyl modules $W^{\\lambda}$ for $\\lambda\\in\\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$ ([[def-type-a-q-schur-algebra-and-weyl-modules]]). Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional right $S$-module and let $(a_{\\lambda})_{\\lambda\\in\\Lambda^{+}(d,n)}$ be integers such that $$\\dim_F\\bigl(M\\varphi_{T^{\\nu}T^{\\nu}}\\bigr)=\\sum_{\\lambda\\in\\Lambda^{+}(d,n)}a_{\\lambda}\\,\\dim_F\\bigl(W^{\\lambda}\\varphi_{T^{\\nu}T^{\\nu}}\\bigr)$$ for every partition $\\nu\\in\\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$, where $\\varphi_{T^{\\nu}T^{\\nu}}$ are the diagonal semistandard basis elements ([[def-type-a-q-schur-algebra-and-weyl-modules]]). Then $[M]=\\sum_{\\lambda}a_{\\lambda}[W^{\\lambda}]$ in the Grothendieck group $G_0(S\\text{-mod})$ ([[def-grothendieck-group-of-an-essentially-small-abelian-category]]). Consequently equality of all semistandard weight-space dimensions forces equality in the Grothendieck group.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Exactness of a weight projection. Fix an idempotent $p=p_\\nu\\in S$. For a short exact sequence $0\\to A\\to B\\xrightarrow{f}C\\to0$ of right $S$-modules, the sequence $0\\to Ap\\to Bp\\to Cp\\to0$ is exact as vector spaces. Injectivity and the kernel identity follow from the underlying sequence and $a=ap$ for $a\\in Ap$. For surjectivity, lift $c\\in C$ to $b\\in B$; then $f(bp)=cp$. Right multiplication by $p$ is a linear projection, and preserves every $S$-submodule, although it need not be an $S$-linear endomorphism when $p$ is not central. Taking dimensions proves additivity. Only the canonical partition weight projections are used. [given, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 Weight functionals on composition series. Apply step 1.1 to every short exact sequence of a finite composition series of $N$. Its factors are the simple modules $L^\\mu$, so $$\\dim_F(Np_\\nu)=\\sum_\\mu[N:L^\\mu]\\dim_F(L^\\mu p_\\nu).$$ The defining relations of the Grothendieck group similarly give $[N]=\\sum_\\mu[N:L^\\mu][L^\\mu]$. This argument requires exactness on vector spaces, not the false assertion that every noncentral weight projection lies in $\\operatorname{End}_S(N)$. [step 1.1, F1, F3]\n\n3.1 Invertibility of the weight matrix: put $X_{\\mu\\nu}:=\\dim_F(L^{\\mu}\\varphi_{T^{\\nu}T^{\\nu}})$ for $\\mu,\\nu\\in\\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$. Feeding a composition series of $W^{\\lambda}$ into step 2.1 gives $$K_{\\lambda\\nu}=\\dim_F(W^{\\lambda}\\varphi_{T^{\\nu}T^{\\nu}})=\\sum_{\\mu}d_{\\lambda\\mu}X_{\\mu\\nu},$$ that is, $K=dX$ with $d=(d_{\\lambda\\mu})$ and $K=(K_{\\lambda\\nu})$. Both $d$ and $K$ are unitriangular over $\\mathbb Z$ by [F1] and [F2] respectively, hence invertible over $\\mathbb Z$, and therefore $X=d^{-1}K$ is invertible over $\\mathbb Z$. [step 2.1, F1, F2]\n\n4.1 Conclusion: write $c_{\\mu}:=[M:L^{\\mu}]$ and $b_{\\mu}:=c_{\\mu}-\\sum_{\\lambda}a_{\\lambda}d_{\\lambda\\mu}\\in\\mathbb Z$. By step 2.1 applied to $M$ and to the identity $\\dim_F(W^{\\lambda}\\varphi_{T^{\\nu}T^{\\nu}})=K_{\\lambda\\nu}$, for every $\\nu$ $$0=\\dim_F(M\\varphi_{T^{\\nu}T^{\\nu}})-\\sum_{\\lambda}a_{\\lambda}\\dim_F(W^{\\lambda}\\varphi_{T^{\\nu}T^{\\nu}})=\\sum_{\\mu}c_{\\mu}X_{\\mu\\nu}-\\sum_{\\lambda}a_{\\lambda}\\sum_{\\mu}d_{\\lambda\\mu}X_{\\mu\\nu}=\\sum_{\\mu}b_{\\mu}X_{\\mu\\nu}.$$ Since $X$ is invertible over $\\mathbb Z$ by step 3.1, $b_{\\mu}=0$ for all $\\mu$. Hence $c_{\\mu}=\\sum_{\\lambda}a_{\\lambda}d_{\\lambda\\mu}$, and step 2.1 together with the defining relations [F3] gives $$[M]=\\sum_{\\mu}c_{\\mu}[L^{\\mu}]=\\sum_{\\lambda}a_{\\lambda}\\sum_{\\mu}d_{\\lambda\\mu}[L^{\\mu}]=\\sum_{\\lambda}a_{\\lambda}[W^{\\lambda}].$$ [step 2.1, step 3.1, F1, F3]\n\n5.1 Consequence: if two finite-dimensional right $S$-modules $M,N$ satisfy $\\dim_F(M\\varphi_{T^{\\nu}T^{\\nu}})=\\dim_F(N\\varphi_{T^{\\nu}T^{\\nu}})$ for all $\\nu\\in\\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$, then applying the argument of step 4.1 to the difference of their multiplicity vectors shows $\\sum_{\\mu}([M:L^{\\mu}]-[N:L^{\\mu}])X_{\\mu\\nu}=0$ for all $\\nu$, so the invertibility of $X$ gives $[M:L^{\\mu}]=[N:L^{\\mu}]$ for all $\\mu$ and hence $[M]=[N]$ in $G_0(S\\text{-mod})$. [step 3.1, step 4.1, F3] \u220e",
