      {
        "id": "thm-nonzero-cell-heads-classify-the-simple-modules-of-a-cellular-algebra",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Nonzero cell heads classify the simple modules of a cellular algebra",
        "deps": [
          "def-cellular-algebra-basis-and-cell-module",
          "def-composition-series-and-length-of-a-module",
          "def-simple-module",
          "lem-cell-form-is-associative-and-its-radical-is-a-submodule",
          "thm-jordan-holder-theorem-for-modules",
          "thm-murphy-tableau-elements-form-a-cellular-basis"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "statement": "Let $F$ be a field and $A$ a finite-dimensional unital cellular $F$-algebra with\ncellular basis $(C,\\Lambda)$ over the finite poset $\\Lambda$\n([[def-cellular-algebra-basis-and-cell-module]]). For $\\lambda\\in\\Lambda$\nput $D^{\\lambda}:=C^{\\lambda}/\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}$, where\n$\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}$ is the radical of the cell form\n([[lem-cell-form-is-associative-and-its-radical-is-a-submodule]]). Then:\n\n1. if $D^{\\lambda}\\ne0$ then $D^{\\lambda}$ is an absolutely irreducible\n   (absolutely simple) $A$-module: it is simple over $F$ and remains simple\n   after every extension of scalars to a larger field;\n2. $\\{D^{\\lambda}:\\lambda\\in\\Lambda,\\ D^{\\lambda}\\ne0\\}$ is a complete set of\n   pairwise non-isomorphic simple $A$-modules ([[def-simple-module]],\n   [[thm-jordan-holder-theorem-for-modules]]);\n3. $D^{\\lambda}\\ne0$ if and only if the cell form\n   $\\langle\\ ,\\ \\rangle_\\lambda$ is not identically zero, if and only if\n   $\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}\\ne C^{\\lambda}$; and, when these equivalent\n   conditions hold, $\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}$ is the unique maximal proper\n   submodule and the Jacobson radical of $C^{\\lambda}$, so $D^{\\lambda}$ is its\n   simple head. If the form is identically zero, the cell-form quotient\n   $D^{\\lambda}$ is zero; no assertion about the Jacobson radical or the actual\n   head of $C^{\\lambda}$ is made.\n\nNo semisimplicity of $A$ is assumed; the theorem is the Graham-Lehrer\nclassification and applies in particular to $A=H_{F,q}(S_n)$ for every field\n$F$ and every $q\\in F^{\\times}$.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 For $\\lambda\\in\\Lambda$ the spans $A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}$ and $A^{\\rhd\\lambda}$ are two-sided ideals: by [F2] both $c^{\\lambda}_{st}a$ and $ac^{\\lambda}_{st}$ are congruent modulo $A^{\\rhd\\lambda}$ to combinations of the $c^{\\lambda}$-elements, so $A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}\\cdot A\\subseteq A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}$ and $A\\cdot A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}\\subseteq A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}$, and $A^{\\rhd\\lambda}$, the sum of the ideals $A^{\\unrhd\\mu}$ over $\\mu\\rhd\\lambda$, is a two-sided ideal as well. Consequently $A^{\\unrhd\\nu}A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}\\subseteq A^{\\unrhd\\nu}\\cap A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}$ and $A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}A^{\\unrhd\\nu}\\subseteq A^{\\unrhd\\nu}\\cap A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}$, because a product of a $c^{\\nu}$-element and a $c^{\\lambda}$-element lies in $A^{\\unrhd\\nu}$ by the ideal property of $A^{\\unrhd\\nu}$ and in $A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}$ by that of $A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}$. The intersection $A^{\\unrhd\\nu}\\cap A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}$ is spanned by the $c^{\\gamma}$ with $\\gamma\\unrhd\\nu$ and $\\gamma\\unrhd\\lambda$; if $\\lambda\\not\\unrhd\\nu$ then no such $\\gamma$ equals $\\lambda$, since $\\gamma=\\lambda$ would force $\\lambda\\unrhd\\nu$, so all of them satisfy $\\gamma\\rhd\\lambda$ and $A^{\\unrhd\\nu}\\cap A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}\\subseteq A^{\\rhd\\lambda}$. Hence, when $\\lambda\\not\\unrhd\\nu$, every product $c^{\\lambda}_{tt}b$ with $b\\in A^{\\unrhd\\nu}$ lies in $A^{\\rhd\\lambda}$; the right action of $b$ on $C^{\\lambda}$ is read off from $c^{\\lambda}_{tt}b\\equiv\\sum_vr_vc^{\\lambda}_{tv}\\pmod{A^{\\rhd\\lambda}}$ with unique coefficients $r_v$, so $b$ acts as the zero operator on $C^{\\lambda}$, that is, $A^{\\unrhd\\nu}\\cdot C^{\\lambda}=0$. [given, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 Generation by vectors outside the radical: let $x\\in C^{\\lambda}$ with $x\\notin\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}$. Since $\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}=\\{z:\\langle z,-\\rangle_\\lambda=0\\}$ there is $y\\in C^{\\lambda}$ with $\\langle x,y\\rangle_\\lambda\\ne0$, and after rescaling $y$ we may assume $\\langle x,y\\rangle_\\lambda=1$. Write $y=\\sum_ua_uc^{\\lambda}_u$ and put $y_v:=\\sum_ua_uc^{\\lambda}_{uv}\\in A$ for $v\\in T(\\lambda)$. Then [F1] gives $xy_v=\\sum_ua_u(xc^{\\lambda}_{uv})=\\sum_ua_u\\langle x,c^{\\lambda}_u\\rangle_\\lambda c^{\\lambda}_v=\\langle x,y\\rangle_\\lambda c^{\\lambda}_v=c^{\\lambda}_v$, so every basis vector of $C^{\\lambda}$ lies in the submodule $xA$, hence $xA=C^{\\lambda}$. [given, F1, algebra]\n\n1.3 Nontrivial action of the upward ideal: suppose $D^{\\mu}\\ne0$. Since $\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\mu}\\ne C^{\\mu}$ there is $t\\in T(\\mu)$ with $c^{\\mu}_t\\notin\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\mu}$; by definition of the radical there is $u\\in T(\\mu)$ with $\\langle c^{\\mu}_t,c^{\\mu}_u\\rangle_\\mu\\ne0$, and then $c^{\\mu}_u\\notin\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\mu}$ as well. By [F1] the element $c^{\\mu}_{ut}\\in A^{\\unrhd\\mu}$ acts on $C^{\\mu}$ by $c^{\\mu}_t\\cdot c^{\\mu}_{ut}=\\langle c^{\\mu}_t,c^{\\mu}_u\\rangle_\\mu c^{\\mu}_t$, so on the quotient $D^{\\mu}$ it sends the nonzero class of $c^{\\mu}_t$ to its nonzero multiple $\\langle c^{\\mu}_t,c^{\\mu}_u\\rangle_\\mu[c^{\\mu}_t]\\ne0$. Thus $A^{\\unrhd\\mu}$ does not annihilate $D^{\\mu}$. [given, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 Consequences for the radical: every proper submodule $M\\subsetneq C^{\\lambda}$ is contained in $\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}$, since $x\\in M$ with $x\\notin\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}$ would give $C^{\\lambda}=xA\\subseteq M$ by step 1.2, a contradiction. Hence, if $\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}\\ne C^{\\lambda}$, then $\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}$ contains every proper submodule, is the unique maximal proper submodule, and equals the Jacobson radical of $C^{\\lambda}$; then $D^{\\lambda}=C^{\\lambda}/\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}$ is simple, because a nonzero submodule of the quotient has the form $N/\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}$ with $N\\supsetneq\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}$ a submodule, and $N=C^{\\lambda}$ by maximality. Moreover $D^{\\lambda}\\ne0$ if and only if $\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}\\ne C^{\\lambda}$ if and only if the form $\\langle-,-\\rangle_\\lambda$ is not identically zero: the radical is the kernel of $x\\mapsto\\langle x,-\\rangle_\\lambda$, so it is all of $C^{\\lambda}$ exactly when $\\langle x,y\\rangle_\\lambda=0$ for all $x,y$, and otherwise this kernel is a proper subspace containing $0$. [given, step 1.2, F1, F3]\n\n2.2 Pairwise distinctness: suppose $D^{\\lambda}\\cong D^{\\mu}\\ne0$. If $\\lambda$ does not dominate $\\mu$, then step 1.1 gives $A^{\\unrhd\\mu}\\cdot C^{\\lambda}=0$, hence $A^{\\unrhd\\mu}\\cdot D^{\\lambda}=0$, and transporting this along the isomorphism gives $A^{\\unrhd\\mu}\\cdot D^{\\mu}=0$, contradicting step 1.3. Hence $\\lambda\\unrhd\\mu$; the same argument with the roles of $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ exchanged gives $\\mu\\unrhd\\lambda$, so $\\lambda=\\mu$ by antisymmetry of the poset. Thus the nonzero cell heads are pairwise non-isomorphic. [step 1.1, step 1.3]\n\n3.1 Absolute irreducibility: let $F'/F$ be a field extension and let $C'^{\\lambda}=C^{\\lambda}\\otimes_FF'$ be the base-changed cell module of the base-changed cellular algebra $A'=A\\otimes_FF'$. The cellular axioms and the cell form base change, so the Gram matrix of the form on $C'^{\\lambda}$ is the scalar extension of the Gram matrix on $C^{\\lambda}$; the rank of a matrix is the largest size of a minor with nonzero determinant, a field-independent computation with the polynomial determinants of the entries, so $\\dim_{F'}\\mathrm{rad}\\,C'^{\\lambda}=\\dim_F\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda}$. If $D^{\\lambda}\\ne0$ then $D'^{\\lambda}:=C'^{\\lambda}/\\mathrm{rad}\\,C'^{\\lambda}\\cong D^{\\lambda}\\otimes_FF'$ is nonzero, and steps 1.2 and 2.1 applied over $F'$ show that every element of $C'^{\\lambda}$ outside $\\mathrm{rad}\\,C'^{\\lambda}$ generates $C'^{\\lambda}$, so $\\mathrm{rad}\\,C'^{\\lambda}$ is the unique maximal submodule of $C'^{\\lambda}$ and $D'^{\\lambda}$ is simple over $F'$. Hence $D^{\\lambda}$ remains simple after every extension of scalars, i.e. it is absolutely irreducible. [given, step 1.2, step 2.1, F1, algebra]\n\n3.2 Completeness: every simple right $A$-module $S$ is isomorphic to some $D^{\\lambda}$ with $D^{\\lambda}\\ne0$. Order $\\Lambda$ as $\\lambda_1,\\dots,\\lambda_N$ so that $\\lambda_i\\rhd\\lambda_j$ implies $i<j$, so larger labels come first, and put $A_j:=\\langle c^{\\lambda_i}_{uv}:i\\le j\\rangle_F$; then each $A_j$ is a two-sided ideal (if $\\mu$ occurs among $\\lambda_1,\\dots,\\lambda_j$ and $\\gamma\\unrhd\\mu$, then $\\gamma$ occurs there too, so $A^{\\unrhd\\mu}\\subseteq A_j$ and the claim follows from step 1.1), $A_0=0$, $A_N=A$. Moreover $A_j/A_{j-1}\\cong A^{\\unrhd\\lambda_j}/A^{\\rhd\\lambda_j}$, which as a right $A$-module is the direct sum over $s\\in T(\\lambda_j)$ of the column spans $\\langle c^{\\lambda_j}_{sv}+A^{\\rhd\\lambda_j}:v\\in T(\\lambda_j)\\rangle\\cong C^{\\lambda_j}$ (the right action preserves the first index by [F2], and $A^{\\rhd\\lambda_j}\\subseteq A_{j-1}$ while $A^{\\unrhd\\lambda_j}\\cap A_{j-1}=A^{\\rhd\\lambda_j}$). Let $S\\ne0$ be simple and let $j$ be least with $SA_j\\ne0$ (such $j$ exists since $SA_N=SA=S$ by unitality). Then $SA_j$ is a nonzero submodule of $S$, so $SA_j=S$, while $SA_{j-1}=0$ by minimality of $j$; choosing $s\\in S$ with $sA_j\\ne0$, the map $A_j/A_{j-1}\\to S$, $a+A_{j-1}\\mapsto sa$, is a well-defined nonzero $A$-module surjection. As $A_j/A_{j-1}$ is a direct sum of copies of $C^{\\lambda_j}$, the images of these copies sum to $S\\ne0$, so some copy surjects onto the simple module $S$; thus $S$ is a simple quotient of $C^{\\lambda_j}$. If the form on $C^{\\lambda_j}$ were identically zero, then $c^{\\lambda_j}_{st}c^{\\lambda_j}_{uv}\\equiv0\\pmod{A^{\\rhd\\lambda_j}}$ for all $s,t,u,v$ by [F1]; since $A^{\\unrhd\\lambda_j}A^{\\unrhd\\lambda_i}\\subseteq A^{\\unrhd\\lambda_i}\\subseteq A_{j-1}$ for $i<j$ and $A^{\\unrhd\\lambda_j}A^{\\rhd\\lambda_j}+A^{\\rhd\\lambda_j}A^{\\unrhd\\lambda_j}\\subseteq A^{\\rhd\\lambda_j}\\subseteq A_{j-1}$, this gives $A_j^2\\subseteq A_{j-1}$, whence $S=SA_j=SA_j^2\\subseteq SA_{j-1}=0$, a contradiction. Hence the form on $C^{\\lambda_j}$ is not identically zero, so by step 2.1 $\\mathrm{rad}\\,C^{\\lambda_j}$ is the unique maximal submodule and the simple quotient $S$ of $C^{\\lambda_j}$ satisfies $S\\cong D^{\\lambda_j}$ with $D^{\\lambda_j}\\ne0$. [given, step 1.1, step 2.1, F1, F2, F3]\n\n4.1 Assembly: part 1 is step 3.1; part 2 is step 3.2 (exhaustion) together with step 2.2 (pairwise non-isomorphism) and step 2.1 (each nonzero $D^{\\lambda}$ is simple); part 3 is step 2.1. The proof classifies right modules, and since the anti-automorphism $i$ identifies right and left $A$-modules, the statement holds on either side. The classification applies to $A=H_{F,q}(S_n)$ because the Murphy basis of [[thm-murphy-tableau-elements-form-a-cellular-basis]] is a cellular basis of the type-$A$ Hecke algebra over every field. No semisimplicity was assumed, all posets and tableau sets are finite, and no choice principle is used. [step 2.1, step 2.2, step 3.1, step 3.2] \u220e",
