        "dependency_level": 10,
        "statement": "Let $R$ be a principal ideal domain ([[def-principal-ideal-domain]]) with a\nchosen prime element $\\pi$, let $F:=R/\\pi R$ be the residue field, and let $M$\nbe a free $R$-module of finite rank\n([[def-free-module-on-a-set-and-standard-basis]]) equipped with a symmetric\n$R$-bilinear form $\\langle\\ ,\\ \\rangle:M\\times M\\to R$ whose extension to the\nfraction field $K=\\operatorname{Frac}(R)$ is non-degenerate (equivalently\n$\\det G\\ne0$ for a Gram matrix $G$ of the form). For $i\\ge1$ put\n$$M(i):=\\{x\\in M:\\langle x,y\\rangle\\in\\pi^iR\\ \\text{for all }y\\in M\\},\\qquad M_F(i):=(M(i)+\\pi M)/\\pi M\\subseteq M_F:=M\\otimes_RF.$$\nThen $M(0)=M$, the $M(i)$ are $R$-submodules with $M(i+1)\\subseteq M(i)$, the\ndescending chain\n$$M_F=M_F(0)\\supseteq M_F(1)\\supseteq M_F(2)\\supseteq\\cdots$$\nstabilises at $0$ (that is, $M_F(z)=0$ for all sufficiently large $z$, because\n$\\det G\\ne0$), and $M_F(1)=\\mathrm{rad}\\,M_F$, the radical of the reduced\nform. This finite descending filtration is the **Jantzen filtration** of $M$\nwith respect to $(R,\\pi,\\langle\\ ,\\ \\rangle)$. The hypotheses are exactly: $R$\na PID, $\\pi$ a prime element, the form non-degenerate over\n$\\operatorname{Frac}(R)$; no valuation-ring or DVR hypothesis is substituted,\nand the prime element $\\pi$, its ideal $\\pi R$ and the fraction field $K$ are\nkept distinct. Each $M(i)$ is a submodule: for the cell forms on this page the\nassociativity identity $\\langle xa,y\\rangle=\\langle x,ya^*\\rangle$ shows\nclosedness under the action. For $0\\ne r\\in R$, define $\\nu_\\pi(r)=\\max\\{k\\ge0:\\pi^k\\mid r\\}$. This maximum is finite: otherwise the uniquely determined elements $r/\\pi^k$ give an ascending chain of ideals whose union is principal, say generated by an element in its $N$-th ideal. The chain then stabilizes at $N$, whereas $(r/\\pi^N)=(r/\\pi^{N+1})$ and cancellation would make $\\pi$ a unit. For termination, put $a=\\nu_\\pi(\\det G)$. If $x\\in M(i)$, its coordinate column $v$ satisfies $Gv\\in\\pi^iR^d$, so the adjugate identity gives $(\\det G)v\\in\\pi^iR^d$. For $i>a$, primality of $\\pi$ forces every coordinate of $v$ into $\\pi R$. Thus $M(i)\\subseteq\\pi M$ and $M_F(i)=0$. In rank zero the filtration is already zero. Finally a reduced vector belongs to the reduced form radical exactly when any lift pairs with every vector of $M$ into $\\pi R$, which is exactly membership in $M(1)$. No choice principle is used.",
