        "dependency_level": 12,
        "statement": "Let $F$ be a field, $q\\in F^{\\times}$, let\n$e=e(F,q)\\in\\{2,3,\\dots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ be the quantum characteristic\n([[def-quantum-characteristic-of-an-invertible-hecke-parameter]]) and let\n$H=H_{F,q}(S_n)$ for $n\\ge0$. For $\\lambda\\vdash n$ let $S^{\\lambda}$ be the\nHecke Specht module and $D^{\\lambda}=S^{\\lambda}/\\mathrm{rad}\\,S^{\\lambda}$ its\ncell-form quotient ([[def-type-a-hecke-specht-module-and-cell-form]]). Then:\n\n1. $D^{\\lambda}\\ne0$ if and only if $\\lambda$ is $e$-regular\n   ([[def-e-regular-and-e-restricted-partitions]]);\n2. $\\{D^{\\lambda}:\\lambda\\vdash n\\text{ is }e\\text{-regular}\\}$ is a complete\n   set of pairwise non-isomorphic absolutely irreducible $H$-modules;\n3. $H$ is split semisimple if and only if $e>n$ (including $e=\\infty$); when\n   $e\\le n$ the algebra has nonzero radical.\n\nThis is the exact root-of-unity replacement for the ordinary Specht\nclassification. At $q=1$ in characteristic $p>0$ it specialises to the\nclassification of the simple $F[S_n]$-modules by $p$-regular partitions\n([[thm-modular-simple-modules-of-sn-are-the-p-regular-specht-heads]]). No\nsemisimplicity is assumed and no choice principle is used.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Use finite joint-primary projections without presupposing which labels survive. The regular right module has a cellular filtration with quotients copies of the cells; refining their common flags in [F3] gives a simultaneous triangular flag on it. Thus each $L_a$ is annihilated by a polynomial $\\prod_b(X-b)^N$, with finitely many diagonal values $b\\in F$ and $N\\ge\\dim H$. Polynomial Bezout and the Chinese remainder construction give $p_{a,b}$ congruent to one modulo $(X-b)^N$ and zero modulo the other factors. Substituting $L_a$ gives commuting idempotents. For a joint word $\\xi$, their product $P_\\xi$ is idempotent in $H$ and on a cell flag has diagonal one exactly at tableaux with word $\\xi$, zero elsewhere. An idempotent has zero image if and only if all these diagonal entries vanish: its triangular matrix would otherwise be nilpotent and idempotent, hence zero. The vector-space functor $M\\mapsto MP_\\xi$ is exact on $H$-modules, because a lift $x$ of $y=yP_\\xi$ can be replaced by $xP_\\xi$, and kernels are preserved. No centrality or $H$-submodule assertion for this image is needed. [F3, F5, given, construct, algebra]\n\n1.2 For finite $e$, equality of two diagonal values $[a]_q,[b]_q$ is equivalent to $a\\equiv b\\pmod e$. Indeed $[a]_q-[b]_q=q^b[a-b]_q$ for all integers, including negative integers. When $q\\ne1$, $e$ is the multiplicative order of $q$, and when $q=1$, $e=\\operatorname{char}F$ and the q-integer is the integer reduced to $F$. Thus a joint word is exactly a residue word in the node convention $c-r\\pmod e$. [given, F3, F6, algebra]\n\n1.3 Prove the complete ladder-boundary comparison. Put $L(s,c)=(e-1)s+c-e$. Suppose $\\alpha$ is restricted, every occupied node has $L<l$, and the addable ladder-$l$ set is nonempty. Set $C=l+e-1$, $T=\\lfloor C/(e-1)\\rfloor$, $d=C-(e-1)T\\in\\{0,\\ldots,e-2\\}$ and $u_s=C-(e-1)s$. For $1\\le s\\le T$, $0\\le\\alpha_s\\le u_s$, and no positive row occurs beyond $T$. The deficits $h_s=u_s-\\alpha_s$ are nonnegative and satisfy $h_{s+1}-h_s=\\alpha_s-\\alpha_{s+1}-(e-1)\\le0$. An addable ladder-$l$ row has deficit zero; thereafter every deficit is zero. Consecutive such rows have width difference $e-1>0$, so the ladder addables form a terminal interval $s_0,\\ldots,T$. A first equality row that is not addable simply precedes this interval. Adding its bottommost $k$ nodes therefore adds in rows $T-k+1,\\ldots,T$. The length of $\\alpha$ is $T$ for $d>0$ and $T-1$ for $d=0$, including the empty case $T=1,d=0$. If $\\beta\\unrhd\\alpha$ has the same size, its length is at most that of $\\alpha$, by the prefix inequality at that length. For $d=0$ all addable rows of $\\beta$ are at most $T$. For $d>0$ the sole possible extra row is $T+1$, with residue $-T$, different from $i=l\\bmod e=d+1-T\\bmod e$ since $1\\le d+1<e$. Hence every addable $i$-node of $\\beta$ lies in a row at most $T$. If $\\gamma$ adds any $k$ of these initial addables, at row cutoff $r$ its prefix increment is at least $\\max(0,k-\\max(T-r,0))$, exactly the increment for the bottommost-$k$ extension $\\delta$ of $\\alpha$. Adding $\\beta$'s prefix inequalities proves $\\gamma\\unrhd\\delta$. This holds for every $k$ simultaneously and needs no same-content premise. [given, construct, algebra]\n\n1.4 The integral intersection factorization. Work over $A=\\mathbb Z[Q,Q^{-1}]$. Suppose a row-standard tableau $t$ already agrees with $t^\\eta$ on rows before $j$, and let $B$ be the canonical label interval of row $j$. In rows $r\\ge j$ let $\\beta_r$ count its $B$-labels. First rearrange labels inside $B$ into increasing row-reading order, then do the same with all remaining tail labels, preserving the number of labels from each interval in every row. This gives a tableau $v$ whose row $r$ consists of a $B$-segment of length $\\beta_r$ followed by a tail-segment of length $\\eta_r-\\beta_r$. The sorting removes inversions separately within the two label intervals, so $d(t)=d(v)wa$ with additive length, $w\\in S_B$, and $a$ a permutation of the remaining tail labels. The intervals are disjoint, so $T_a$ commutes with $x_B$, and $T_wx_B=Q^{\\ell(w)}x_B$. Let $K'$ be the subgroup cutting each canonical row $r\\ge j$ into lengths $\\beta_r,\\eta_r-\\beta_r$, while retaining the earlier rows. Let $K$ cut $B$ into consecutive lengths $\\beta_r$ and the tail into consecutive lengths $\\eta_r-\\beta_r$, again retaining earlier rows. The permutation $d(v)$ increases on these segments on both sides and transports $K'$ to $K$. Hence $x_{K'}T_{d(v)}=T_{d(v)}x_K$, by expansion into length-additive standard-basis words. If $E$ consists of the minimal representatives with $S_\\eta=\\bigsqcup_{u\\in E}uK'$, then $x_\\eta=\\sum_{u\\in E}T_ux_{K'}$. Write $\\nu=(\\eta_1,\\ldots,\\eta_{j-1},\\eta_j,\\eta_j-\\beta_j,\\ldots,\\eta_k-\\beta_k)$, omitting zero parts. Multiplication of $x_K$ by $x_B$ gives $[\\beta]!_Qx_\\nu$: the $B$-intersection subgroup contributes $\\sum Q^{\\ell(b)}=[\\beta]!_Q$, while its tail and earlier-row factors commute with $x_B$. Consequently the exact identity is $x_\\eta T_{d(t)}x_B=Q^{\\ell(w)}[\\beta]!_Q\\sum_{u\\in E}T_uT_{d(v)}x_\\nu T_a$. No scalar is divided out. Sorting $\\nu$ to a partition uses the invertible block-reordering identity in [F5], so this is in $[\\beta]!_Q Hx_{\\nu^+}H$. If $\\nu^+\\ntrianglelefteq\\eta$, the dominance-ideal assertion in [F5] makes its shape-$\\eta$ part vanish. Otherwise the first $j+1$ largest parts of $\\nu$ have sum at least $\\eta_1+\\cdots+\\eta_j+\\eta_j-\\beta_j$; comparison with $\\eta$ gives $\\beta_j\\ge\\eta_j-\\eta_{j+1}$. [F5, construct, algebra]\n\n2.1 Canonical-row support without factorial cancellation. The semistandard permutation-basis theorem [[lem-type-a-hecke-permutation-modules-have-semistandard-murphy-bases-and-specht-filtrations]], followed by star, gives an exact coordinate condition for $Hx_\\rho$: every supported right Murphy tableau has semistandard type $\\rho$. Apply it to the factor remaining after $[\\beta]!_Q$ in step 1.4, before putting back $T_a$. This factor belongs to $Hx_\\nu$. In shape $\\eta$, semistandardness forces the first $\\eta_1$ labels into the first row, the next $\\eta_2$ into the second, and so on through $B$: a label cannot occur below its own row, and each earlier row is already full. Thus its right tableaux have the first $j$ canonical rows. The permutation $a$ uses only later labels. Expanding $T_a$ by the row action and straightening cannot alter these canonical rows: their prefix counts are maximal, and same-shape straightening increases every tableau-prefix count by that supplier's Statement. This proves the canonical-row assertion even for the quotient by the displayed factorial. Starting at $j=1$, repeat steps 1.4 and 2.1 for each surviving standard-tableau term. If $\\eta_j-\\eta_{j+1}\\ge e$, every surviving term at that stage has $\\beta_j\\ge e$, so $[e]!_Q$ divides its factor $[\\beta]!_Q$. Later multiplication and integral straightening preserve this common divisor. This establishes the full repeated-symmetrizer support and divisibility used below. [F5, step 1.4, algebra]\n\n2.2 For restricted $\\eta$, number its nodes by increasing $L$, taking lower rows first on a tie, to obtain $t_\\eta$. Moving right increases $L$ by one and moving down by $e-1$, so it is standard. The diagrams $\\alpha_l$ of nodes with $L<l$ and $\\delta_l$ with $L\\le l$ are restricted: their row widths are the minimum of the restricted widths of $\\eta$ and the positive part of the affine cutoff bound, each having adjacent difference at most $e-1$. A node on ladder $l$ forces every lower positive position on that ladder to be occupied, since $\\eta_{s+1}\\ge\\eta_s-(e-1)$. Thus $\\delta_l$ adds the bottommost $a_l$ ladder addables to $\\alpha_l$. Any competing standard tableau with the same full residue word has, in the corresponding block, $a_l$ additions all of residue $l$. Every such node was addable before the block: adding an $i$-node creates new addable corners only of residues $i+1,i-1$, distinct from $i$ for $e\\ge2$. Starting with the empty diagrams, step 1.3 inductively shows its shape dominates $\\delta_l$ at every completed block, and hence its final shape dominates $\\eta$. No strictly lower shape has the word of $t_\\eta$, and no same-shape tableau uniqueness is asserted. [step 1.3, construct, algebra]\n\n3.1 Choose $P_\\xi$ for the word of $t_\\eta$. It has nonzero image on $C_{\\mathrm M}(\\eta)$ and zero image on every $C_{\\mathrm M}(\\rho)$ with $\\rho\\lhd\\eta$, by steps 1.2 and 2.2. Therefore it has zero image on their form quotients. Apply the exact projection to a composition series of $C_{\\mathrm M}(\\eta)$. Some constituent has nonzero image; by [F1] and [F4] it is a nonzero $D_{\\mathrm M}(\\rho)$ with $\\rho\\unlhd\\eta$. The exclusion forces $\\rho=\\eta$, proving the restricted Murphy quotient nonzero over the original field. This is a joint-word and cellular-support proof, not an import of cellular/crystal label identification. [F1, F4, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.2, algebra]\n\n4.1 If $\\eta_j-\\eta_{j+1}\\ge e$ for some $j$, every surviving term at that row has $\\beta_j\\ge e$ by steps 1.4 and 2.1 and is divisible by $[e]!_Q$. Later multiplication and straightening cannot remove this polynomial factor. Every Gram entry is the coefficient of $x_\\eta$ in $x_\\eta T_{d(s)}T_{d(t)}^*x_\\eta$ modulo higher shapes; first expand its middle factor in the row-module basis and apply the proved row calculation. Thus every entry is divisible by $[e]!_Q$. Specializing $Q$ to $q$ makes that factor zero, so the entire Murphy form vanishes and $D_{\\mathrm M}(\\eta)=0$. This concerns a common divisor of entries, not a product-of-hooks formula for a determinant. Combining with step 3.1 proves the finite-$e$ Murphy criterion. [F1, F5, step 1.4, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n4.2 For $e>n$, including infinity, distinct standard tableaux of any shapes have distinct full diagonal words. At their first unequal step the preceding diagram has size $a-1$; its distinct addable-corner contents differ by a nonzero integer of absolute value at most $a\\le n$, so their q-integers differ. Induction recovers every next node. Consequently every tableau word of $C_{\\mathrm M}(\\eta)$ occurs in no strictly lower cell. Exact projections as in step 3.1 and diagonal multiplicity one in [F4] show that all $\\dim C_{\\mathrm M}(\\eta)$ flag factors occur in $D_{\\mathrm M}(\\eta)$; thus this quotient has the full cell dimension and its form radical is zero. In particular at $e=\\infty$ every partition is restricted and has nonzero quotient. At $n=0$ the unique cell is $F$ with norm one. [F3, F4, step 1.1, step 3.1, construct, algebra]\n\n5.1 Establish the complete semisimplicity clause. Put $f_\\eta=\\dim C_{\\mathrm M}(\\eta)$ and $d_\\eta=\\dim D_{\\mathrm M}(\\eta)$, with zero for a zero quotient. The Murphy basis gives $\\dim H=\\sum_\\eta f_\\eta^2$. On each nonzero quotient its nondegenerate form and the cell law supply all rank-one operators. Since the cells are right modules, their action gives a surjective algebra map $H\\to\\operatorname{End}_F(D_{\\mathrm M}(\\eta))^{\\mathrm{op}}$, a full matrix algebra after transpose. Its kernels are distinct maximal two-sided ideals: equality would give the same matrix-algebra simple module, contradicting [F1]. They are pairwise comaximal. Their finite product map is surjective: for each kernel $I_a$ and each other $I_b$ choose $u_{ab}\\in I_b$ congruent to one modulo $I_a$, multiply over $b\\ne a$, and use these elements to isolate each prescribed matrix component. Its kernel is the radical by the complete simple list and [F6]. Hence $\\dim(H/\\operatorname{rad}H)=\\sum_\\eta d_\\eta^2$. Step 4.2 makes this equal to $\\dim H$ when $e>n$, giving an isomorphism with a product of full matrix algebras over $F$. When finite $e\\le n$, the one-dimensional Murphy row cell has norm $[n]!_q=0$, since $x_{(n)}^2=[n]!_q x_{(n)}$. Its quotient dimension is zero while its cell dimension is one; every other $d_\\eta\\le f_\\eta$. The dimension sum is therefore strictly smaller and $\\operatorname{rad}H\\ne0$. This proves both directions of part 3 without any claim that some Specht module must be reducible. [F1, F5, F6, step 4.2, algebra]\n\n6.1 Apply the same exact involution to every Murphy quotient: $D^\\lambda=D_{\\mathrm M}(\\lambda')^\\theta$ is nonzero exactly when $\\lambda'$ is restricted, equivalently when $\\lambda$ is regular. The nonzero quotients remain pairwise distinct absolutely simple and complete by [F1] and [F2]. This proves parts 1 and 2, and step 5.1 gives part 3. At $q=1$, [F6] gives precisely the ordinary $p$-regular group classification, including characteristic two; no splitting or semisimplicity assumption was made. All selections and polynomial computations were finite, so no choice principle is used. [F1, F2, F6, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 4.2, step 5.1] \u220e",
