        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, inherited only through the integral highest-lattice supplier. Put $A=\\mathbb Z[v,v^{-1}]$, $q=v^2$, $N\\ge1$, and $r\\ge0$. Use the integral $U_A(\\mathfrak{gl}_N)$, its surjection $\\rho:U_A\\to S_A(N,r)$, and $\\tau(E_i^{(a)})=F_i^{(a)}$, $\\tau(K_j)=K_j$ of [[lem-integral-divided-power-tensor-action-surjects-onto-type-a-q-schur-algebra]]. For a partition $\\lambda$ of $r$ with at most $N$ parts, padded by zeros, set $m_i=\\lambda_i-\\lambda_{i+1}$ and\n$$Q_A(\\lambda)=U_A^-/\\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\\sum_{a>m_i}U_A^-F_i^{(a)}.$$\nEquip this quotient with its integral highest-module action and highest vector $z=1+\\sum U_A^-F_i^{(a)}$, with $K_jz=v^{\\lambda_j}z$ and the integral toral binomials acting by the corresponding integer-weight characters. Let $W_A^\\lambda$ be the actual fixed-first-index right Weyl cell of [[def-type-a-q-schur-algebra-and-weyl-modules]], with semistandard basis $w_T$, cellular form $B_{\\rm cell}$ and $h=w_{T^\\lambda}$. Give its underlying module the left action\n$$u\\cdot w=w\\rho(\\tau(u)).$$\nThen the unique highest-vector map $f:Q_A(\\lambda)\\to W_A^\\lambda$, $z\\mapsto h$, is an isomorphism. In particular $b_T=f^{-1}(w_T)$ is a complete integral semistandard basis, of weight $\\operatorname{type}(T)$, and the displayed divided-right-power relations are the entire negative-half presentation. This does not identify $b_T$ with a particular ordered root monomial.\n\nSet $c_\\mu=v^{-\\sum_j\\binom{\\mu_j}{2}}$. There is a unique homogeneous normalized $\\tau$-contravariant form $B$ on $Q_A(\\lambda)$, explicitly\n$$B(xz,yz)=[z](\\tau(x)yz).$$\nHere $[z]$ means projection onto the free highest-weight line followed by its coefficient in $z$. It is integral and symmetric, and for homogeneous $a,b$ of weight $\\mu$ satisfies\n$$B(a,b)=\\frac{c_\\lambda}{c_\\mu}B_{\\rm cell}(f(a),f(b)).$$\nDifferent weights are orthogonal. Every ratio in this formula is a Laurent unit. The module, basis, quotient presentation, highest-coefficient formula and form comparison commute with arbitrary commutative $A$-algebra base change, including fields at roots of unity and singular Gram matrices. Form radicals agree under $f$ in every such fibre; no assertion that radicals commute with arbitrary base change is made.\n\nOver every commutative $A$-algebra $R$, every cyclic left $U_R$-module with nonnegative composition-label support of total degree $r$ and the full integer toral characters, generated by a vector of weight $\\lambda$ killed by every positive divided generator, is a quotient of $Q_R(\\lambda)$. Equivalently, the left Weyl cell is the polynomial highest-induced module $S_R\\otimes_{S_R^+}R_\\lambda$, where $S_R^+$ is the image of the integral toral-positive subalgebra and $R_\\lambda$ is its highest character. This includes every polynomial Schur module and the zero module. The full toral-character label convention is the one specified in [[lem-polynomial-quantum-highest-vectors-satisfy-all-divided-power-relations]]; no factorization through the Schur quotient is assumed for this stronger cyclic-highest assertion.",
