        "dependency_level": 12,
        "statement": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $q\\in R^{\\times}$. In $H=H_{R,q}(S_n)$ the Hecke Jucys-Murphy elements ([[def-hecke-jucys-murphy-elements-and-q-contents]]) for $n\\ge4$ are $$L_1=0,\\quad L_2=q^{-1}T_{(1,2)},\\quad L_3=q^{-1}T_{(2,3)}+q^{-2}T_{(1,3)},\\quad L_4=q^{-1}T_{(3,4)}+q^{-2}T_{(2,4)}+q^{-3}T_{(1,4)},$$ and in general $L_k=\\sum_{j<k}q^{j-k}T_{(j,k)}$. For the standard tableau $t=\\begin{smallmatrix}1&3&4\\\\2\\end{smallmatrix}$ of shape $(3,1)$ the contents of the entries $k=1,2,3,4$ are $0,-1,1,2$ ([[def-content-vector-of-a-standard-tableau]]), so the diagonal eigenvalues of $L_1,\\dots,L_4$ on the Murphy vector $m_t$ are $[0]_q=0$, $[-1]_q=-q^{-1}$, $[1]_q=1$ and $[2]_q=1+q$; the triangular correction terms $\\sum_{v\\rhd t}a_vm_v$ are present in general ([[thm-hecke-jucys-murphy-action-is-dominance-triangular]]) in the Murphy basis, even in the semisimple range. In the separated range, replacing $m_t$ by the seminormal vector $f_t$ removes these correction terms, as proved in [[thm-separated-hecke-residues-give-seminormal-idempotents-and-bases]]. At $q=1$ the four elements specialize to the ordinary Jucys-Murphy elements $X_1=0$, $X_2=(1\\,2)$, $X_3=(1\\,3)+(2\\,3)$, $X_4=(1\\,4)+(2\\,4)+(3\\,4)$ and the eigenvalues to $0,-1,1,2$, the standard contents of $t$.",
        "strategy": "## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** A commutative ring $R$, $q\\in R^{\\times}$, the Hecke algebra $H_{R,q}(S_n)$, the Jucys-Murphy elements and q-contents ([[def-hecke-jucys-murphy-elements-and-q-contents]]), the content vector of a standard tableau ([[def-content-vector-of-a-standard-tableau]]), and the q=1 specialization $H_{R,1}(S_n)\\cong R[S_n]$ ([[def-hecke-jucys-murphy-elements-and-q-contents]]).\n\n[F1] ([[def-hecke-jucys-murphy-elements-and-q-contents]]) $L_k=\\sum_{j<k}q^{j-k}T_{(j,k)}$; $[m]_q=1+q+\\cdots+q^{m-1}$ for $m\\ge0$, $[-m]_q=-q^{-m}[m]_q$ for $m>0$, and $[m]_1=m$ for all $m\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\n[F2] ([[thm-hecke-jucys-murphy-action-is-dominance-triangular]]) $m_tL_k=[\\operatorname{content}_t(k)]_q\\,m_t+\\sum_{v\\rhd t}a_vm_v$ on the Murphy cell module, so the displayed values are the diagonal entries and the correction terms are as stated.\n\n\n\n## Verification\n\n1.1 Substitution: in [F1], for $k=1$ the sum is empty, giving $L_1=0$; for $k=2$ it has the single term $q^{1-2}T_{(1,2)}=q^{-1}T_{(1,2)}$; for $k=3$ it has $q^{-1}T_{(2,3)}+q^{-2}T_{(1,3)}$; and for $k=4$ it has $q^{-1}T_{(3,4)}+q^{-2}T_{(2,4)}+q^{-3}T_{(1,4)}$, matching the displayed list. [given, F1]\n\n1.2 Contents: reading the tableau $t$ with rows $(1,3,4)$ and $(2)$, the node of $1$ is $(1,1)$ with content $1-1=0$, the node of $2$ is $(2,1)$ with content $1-2=-1$, the node of $3$ is $(1,2)$ with content $2-1=1$ and the node of $4$ is $(1,3)$ with content $3-1=2$; by definition $[\\operatorname{content}_t(k)]_q$ is the q-integer of these contents. [given, F1]\n\n2.1 Eigenvalues: by [F1], $[0]_q=0$, $[-1]_q=-q^{-1}[1]_q=-q^{-1}$, $[1]_q=1$ and $[2]_q=1+q$; substituting into the triangularity formula [F2] gives the stated diagonal eigenvalues $0,-1,1,2$ up to the q-powers $-q^{-1},1,1+q$. The correction terms are exactly the sum $\\sum_{v\\rhd t}a_vm_v$ of [F2] and are not asserted to vanish outside the semisimple range. [step 1.1, step 1.2, F1, F2]\n\n3.1 Specialization: setting $q=1$ in the displayed formulas turns $L_k$ into $\\sum_{j<k}(j\\,k)=X_k$ by the specialization isomorphism of [[def-hecke-jucys-murphy-elements-and-q-contents]] and [F1], giving $X_1=0$, $X_2=(1\\,2)$, $X_3=(1\\,3)+(2\\,3)$ and $X_4=(1\\,4)+(2\\,4)+(3\\,4)$; the eigenvalues $[m]_1=m$ become $0,-1,1,2$, the contents of $t$. [step 1.2, step 2.1, F1] \u220e",
